# Python conventions

Docstrings follow PEP 257 and stay short.

1. Every module has a one-line docstring. Add more only when the module needs orientation.
2. Every public class, function and method has a docstring. The first line is one imperative sentence, ends with a period, and fits on one line.
3. Add a body, after a blank line, only when the behaviour is not obvious from the name and signature: side effects, exceptions callers must handle, units, timezones or currency, security behaviour, non-obvious defaults, or idempotency. One to three sentences.
4. Do not restate type hints. No `Args:` or `Returns:` sections that repeat the signature. A short `Raises:` or `Example:` is allowed when it prevents misuse.
5. A private helper needs a docstring only when it is non-trivial. Otherwise a comment can explain why.
6. Do not narrate the implementation. No authorship, history, or TODOs in docstrings. Comments explain why, not what.
7. Use triple double quotes. For a multi-line docstring, put the closing quotes on their own line.
8. Tests need no docstrings. Alembic revision files are exempt.

Logging uses the standard library. Each module uses `logger = logging.getLogger(__name__)`. Log calls use lazy `%s` formatting, not f-strings. Do not use `print` in application code or scripts. Do not log secrets, card numbers, passwords, emails, postcodes, request bodies, or raw prompts. Identify a customer by `customer_id` only.

Repositories are SQL only. Business rules live in services.
